// Class 10 Science practice bank, part 2: chapters 8-13 (Heredity, Physics and Environment).
StudyData.addPractice(10, "science", {
  "heredity": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Who is known as the father of genetics for his work on pea plants?", options: ["Charles Darwin", "Louis Pasteur", "Gregor Mendel", "Robert Hooke"], answer: 2, explain: "Gregor Johann Mendel worked out the laws of inheritance using garden peas.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Mendel carried out his experiments on:", options: ["garden pea plants", "wheat plants", "rice plants", "maize plants"], answer: 0, explain: "He used the garden pea (Pisum sativum) because it has clear contrasting traits and is easy to cross.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The genotype of a pure short pea plant is:", options: ["Tt (one tall, one short gene)", "TT (two tall genes)", "T (a single tall gene)", "tt (two short genes)"], answer: 3, explain: "A recessive trait shows only when both copies are recessive, so a short plant must be tt.", level: 1 },
      { q: "How many pairs of chromosomes are present in a human body cell?", options: ["22", "23", "24", "46"], answer: 1, explain: "A human cell has 23 pairs (46 chromosomes): 22 pairs of autosomes and one pair of sex chromosomes.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The sex chromosomes of a human female are:", options: ["XY", "XO", "XX", "YY"], answer: 2, explain: "Females have two similar sex chromosomes, XX; males have XY.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The trait that appears in the F₁ generation of a cross between two pure contrasting parents is called:", options: ["dominant", "recessive", "acquired", "mutant"], answer: 0, explain: "The trait expressed in F₁ is the dominant trait; the hidden one is recessive.", level: 1 },

      { q: "A tall pea plant (Tt) is crossed with a short pea plant (tt). The progeny will be:", options: ["all tall", "1 tall : 1 short", "3 tall : 1 short", "all short"], answer: 1, explain: "Gametes T, t from one parent and only t from the other give Tt and tt in equal numbers: 1 tall : 1 short.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which statement about the germ cells (gametes) of an organism is correct?", options: ["They carry only the dominant gene of each pair", "They carry a copy of every gene from both parents", "They carry only genes of the mother", "They carry one copy of each gene pair"], answer: 3, explain: "Each gamete has half the chromosome number, so only one copy of each gene pair. Fertilisation restores the pair.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Meena has two daughters. Her relatives blame her for not having a son. Which statement is scientifically correct?", options: ["The sex of the child is decided by whether the father's sperm carries X or Y", "The mother's egg decides the sex of the child", "The sex depends on the mother's diet", "The sex depends on the number of children already born"], answer: 0, explain: "All eggs carry X. A Y-carrying sperm gives a boy and an X-carrying sperm gives a girl, so the father's sperm decides.", level: 2 },
      { q: "In a dihybrid cross between RRYY and rryy pea plants, how many of the 16 F₂ plants (in the ideal ratio) are round and green?", options: ["9", "1", "3", "6"], answer: 2, explain: "The F₂ ratio is 9 round-yellow : 3 round-green : 3 wrinkled-yellow : 1 wrinkled-green.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): In the F₂ generation of a monohybrid cross, the phenotypic ratio is 3 : 1.\nReason (R): The genotypic ratio in the F₂ generation is 1 : 2 : 1.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 1, explain: "Both statements are true. The 3 : 1 phenotypic ratio arises because TT and Tt look the same (tall), but R only states another ratio; it is not the reason by itself.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "A gardener crosses pure violet-flowered and pure white-flowered pea plants. All F₁ plants have violet flowers. He grows 400 plants from the selfed F₁. How many white-flowered plants are expected?", options: ["100", "300", "200", "400"], answer: 0, explain: "White is recessive and appears in one-fourth of F₂: 400 ÷ 4 = 100.", level: 2 },
      { q: "In the F₂ generation of RRYY × rryy, which two phenotypes are new combinations not seen in the parents?", options: ["round-yellow and wrinkled-green", "round-yellow and round-green", "round-green and wrinkled-yellow", "wrinkled-green and round-yellow"], answer: 2, explain: "The parents were round-yellow and wrinkled-green. Round-green and wrinkled-yellow are new combinations, showing independent inheritance.", level: 2 },

      { q: "A tall plant is picked at random from the F₂ generation of a Tt × Tt cross. The chance that it is pure tall (TT) is:", options: ["1/4", "1/2", "1/3", "2/3"], answer: 2, explain: "Among tall plants the genotypes are TT : Tt = 1 : 2, so the chance of TT is 1/3.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A dihybrid cross RRYY × rryy gives 1600 F₂ plants. The expected number of wrinkled-yellow plants is:", options: ["900", "300", "100", "600"], answer: 1, explain: "Wrinkled-yellow is 3/16 of the F₂: 3/16 × 1600 = 300.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): A woman produces two kinds of eggs, one with X and one with Y chromosome.\nReason (R): A man produces two kinds of sperms, one with X and one with Y chromosome.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "A woman is XX, so all her eggs carry X. Only the man (XY) produces two kinds of gametes.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "A human sperm cell contains:", options: ["22 autosomes and either X or Y", "22 autosomes and both X and Y", "23 autosomes and X", "23 pairs of chromosomes"], answer: 0, explain: "A gamete has half the chromosomes (23): 22 autosomes plus one sex chromosome, X or Y.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A tall pea plant is crossed with a short plant and the progeny are 50% tall and 50% short. The genotype of the tall parent must be:", options: ["TT (two tall genes)", "Tt (one tall, one short gene)", "tt (two short genes)", "it cannot be known"], answer: 1, explain: "If the tall parent were TT, all progeny would be tall. Half short means it carried a t allele, so it is Tt.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "What is a monohybrid cross?", a: "A cross between two parents that differ in one pair of contrasting traits, e.g. tall (TT) × short (tt) pea plants.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "State the phenotypic ratio obtained in the F₂ generation of a dihybrid cross between round-yellow and wrinkled-green pea plants.", a: "9 round-yellow : 3 round-green : 3 wrinkled-yellow : 1 wrinkled-green (9 : 3 : 3 : 1).", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Why do all the eggs of a human female carry the same kind of sex chromosome?", a: "A woman has two X chromosomes (XX), so every egg receives one X.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Explain why a recessive trait is not seen in the F₁ generation but reappears in the F₂ generation.", a: "In F₁ every plant is Tt; the dominant gene T masks the recessive t, so the recessive trait is hidden.\nOn selfing, the gametes T and t combine at random, and one-fourth of the F₂ plants get tt, so the recessive trait reappears.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Differentiate between autosomes and sex chromosomes in humans.", a: "Autosomes are the 22 pairs of chromosomes that control body traits other than sex; they are alike in males and females.\nSex chromosomes are the 23rd pair (XX in females, XY in males) and decide the sex of the individual.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "A violet-flowered pea plant (Vv) is crossed with a white-flowered plant (vv). Write the genotypes and phenotypes of the progeny and their ratio. (V = violet, dominant.)", a: "Gametes: Vv gives V and v; vv gives only v.\nProgeny: Vv (violet) and vv (white) in equal numbers.\nRatio: 1 violet : 1 white (genotypic ratio 1 Vv : 1 vv).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "A family has two daughters and the husband blames his wife for not having a son. Using a cross, show who determines the sex of a child and the chance of a boy.", a: "Mother: XX, so all eggs carry X. Father: XY, so half the sperms carry X and half carry Y.\nCross: X sperm + X egg = XX (girl); Y sperm + X egg = XY (boy).\nEach child has a 50% chance of being a boy, decided by the father's sperm, so the wife cannot be blamed.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "In a dihybrid cross between pure round-yellow and pure wrinkled-green pea plants, name the four types of F₂ plants with their ratio. Which two types are new combinations, and what does this show?", a: "Round-yellow : round-green : wrinkled-yellow : wrinkled-green = 9 : 3 : 3 : 1.\nRound-green and wrinkled-yellow are the new combinations (not present in the parents).\nThis shows that the traits for seed shape and seed colour are inherited independently of each other.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "How does the number of chromosomes remain the same in every generation of a sexually reproducing species? Give three points.", a: "Each germ cell (egg or sperm) carries only one set of chromosomes, i.e. half the number found in body cells.\nAt fertilisation the egg and sperm fuse, and the zygote gets one copy of each from each parent.\nThis restores the full number (23 pairs in humans), so the number is constant from generation to generation.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Mendel crossed pure tall (TT) and pure short (tt) pea plants. Show the cross up to the F₂ generation and write the phenotypic and genotypic ratios of F₂.", a: "Parents: TT (tall) × tt (short); gametes T and t.\nF₁: all Tt, all tall (T is dominant).\nF₁ self-pollinated: Tt × Tt; gametes T, t from each parent.\nF₂ combinations: T with T = TT; T with t = Tt; t with T = Tt; t with t = tt.\nGenotypic ratio: 1 TT : 2 Tt : 1 tt.\nPhenotypic ratio: 3 tall : 1 short.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "A pea plant with round yellow seeds (RRYY) is crossed with one with wrinkled green seeds (rryy). Show the F₂ generation in a Punnett square and write the phenotypic ratio. (R = round, Y = yellow are dominant.)", a: "F₁: all RrYy, round yellow. F₁ gametes: RY, Ry, rY, ry.\nF₂ Punnett square (rows and columns in the order RY, Ry, rY, ry):\nRY: RRYY, RRYy, RrYY, RrYy\nRy: RRYy, RRyy, RrYy, Rryy\nrY: RrYY, RrYy, rrYY, rrYy\nry: RrYy, Rryy, rrYy, rryy\nPhenotypes: round yellow 9, round green 3, wrinkled yellow 3, wrinkled green 1.\nRatio = 9 : 3 : 3 : 1.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Case study: A student crossed pure tall pea plants with pure short pea plants. All the F₁ plants were tall. She allowed the F₁ plants to self-pollinate and obtained 200 plants in the F₂ generation.\n(i) What is the genotype of the F₁ plants?\n(ii) How many tall plants and how many short plants are expected in the F₂? How many of the tall plants are expected to be Tt?\n(iii) Why does the short trait reappear in the F₂?", a: "(i) Tt.\n(ii) 3 : 1 ratio, so 150 tall and 50 short. The tall plants are TT : Tt = 1 : 2, so 50 are TT and 100 are Tt.\n(iii) The F₁ plants carry the recessive gene t hidden by T. When two t gametes combine, the tt plant is short, so the trait reappears in one-fourth of the F₂.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "light-reflection-and-refraction": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "A ray of light strikes a plane mirror at an angle of incidence of 40°. The angle of reflection is:", options: ["20°", "40°", "50°", "80°"], answer: 1, explain: "By the first law of reflection, the angle of reflection equals the angle of incidence, 40°.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which mirror is used as a rear-view mirror in vehicles?", options: ["Plane mirror", "Concave mirror", "Cylindrical mirror", "Convex mirror"], answer: 3, explain: "A convex mirror gives an erect, diminished image and a wider field of view.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The SI unit of the power of a lens is:", options: ["dioptre", "metre", "watt", "lumen"], answer: 0, explain: "Power P = 1/f with f in metres; the unit is the dioptre (D).", level: 1 },
      { q: "The radius of curvature of a spherical mirror is 30 cm. Its focal length is:", options: ["60 cm", "30 cm", "15 cm", "7.5 cm"], answer: 2, explain: "f = R/2 = 30/2 = 15 cm (the sign depends on the type of mirror).", level: 1 },
      { q: "The image formed by a concave lens is always:", options: ["virtual, erect and diminished", "real, inverted and diminished", "virtual, erect and enlarged", "real, erect and enlarged"], answer: 0, explain: "A concave lens spreads the rays out, so the image is virtual, erect and smaller, between F and O.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The speed of light in vacuum is nearly:", options: ["3 × 10⁶ m/s", "3 × 10⁸ m/s", "3 × 10¹⁰ m/s", "3 × 10⁵ m/s"], answer: 1, explain: "c = 3 × 10⁸ m/s.", level: 1 },

      { q: "An object is placed at the centre of curvature of a concave mirror. The image formed is:", options: ["virtual, erect and enlarged", "real, inverted and diminished, between F and C", "real, inverted and enlarged, beyond C", "real, inverted and of the same size, at C"], answer: 3, explain: "For an object at C, the image is at C, real, inverted and the same size (m = −1).", level: 2 },
      { q: "The speed of light in a medium is 2 × 10⁸ m/s. The refractive index of the medium is:", options: ["0.67", "2.0", "1.5", "6.0"], answer: 2, explain: "n = c/v = (3 × 10⁸)/(2 × 10⁸) = 1.5.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Riya looks at a pencil kept partly dipped in a glass of water. It appears bent at the water surface. This is because of:", options: ["reflection of light from the pencil", "refraction of light as it passes from water to air", "dispersion of light in water", "scattering of light by water molecules"], answer: 1, explain: "Light from the dipped part bends away from the normal on leaving water, so the pencil appears bent.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A lens has a power of −2.5 D. It is a:", options: ["concave lens of focal length 40 cm", "convex lens of focal length 40 cm", "concave lens of focal length 2.5 cm", "convex lens of focal length 2.5 cm"], answer: 0, explain: "Negative power means a concave lens; f = 1/P = 1/(−2.5) = −0.4 m = −40 cm.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): A dentist uses a concave mirror to examine teeth.\nReason (R): A concave mirror held close to an object forms a virtual, erect and enlarged image.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "With the tooth between P and F, the image is virtual, erect and magnified, which helps the dentist see it clearly.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "A student holds a lens facing the Sun and gets the smallest, brightest spot on a paper 12 cm from the lens. The lens and its power are:", options: ["concave, −8.3 D", "convex, +12 D", "convex, +0.12 D", "convex, +8.3 D"], answer: 3, explain: "A lens that focuses sunlight is convex with f = 12 cm = 0.12 m, so P = 1/0.12 ≈ +8.3 D.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A mirror forms an image with magnification m = +1/3. The image is:", options: ["real, inverted and one-third of the object size", "virtual, erect and one-third of the object size", "virtual, erect and three times the object size", "real, erect and three times the object size"], answer: 1, explain: "Positive m means virtual and erect; the value 1/3 means smaller (diminished). It could be a convex mirror.", level: 2 },

      { q: "An object is 18 cm in front of a concave mirror of focal length 12 cm. The image formed is:", options: ["virtual, erect, 36 cm behind the mirror", "real, inverted, 36 cm in front of the mirror, same size as the object", "real, inverted, 36 cm in front of the mirror, twice the size of the object", "real, inverted, 7.2 cm in front of the mirror, diminished"], answer: 2, explain: "u = −18, f = −12: 1/v = 1/f − 1/u = −1/12 + 1/18 = −1/36, so v = −36 cm. m = −v/u = −(−36)/(−18) = −2: real, inverted, twice the size.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A 5 cm tall object is placed 30 cm from a convex lens of focal length 20 cm. The size and nature of the image are:", options: ["2.5 cm, inverted", "2.5 cm, erect", "10 cm, erect", "10 cm, inverted"], answer: 3, explain: "1/v = 1/20 − 1/30 = 1/60, so v = +60 cm. m = v/u = 60/(−30) = −2, so h′ = −2 × 5 = −10 cm: 10 cm tall and inverted.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A convex mirror has a focal length of 20 cm. An object is placed 60 cm in front of it. The image distance (New Cartesian convention) is:", options: ["+30 cm", "−15 cm", "+15 cm", "−30 cm"], answer: 2, explain: "f = +20, u = −60: 1/v = 1/20 + 1/60 = 4/60, so v = +15 cm, a virtual image behind the mirror.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The speed of light in water is greater than in glass.\nReason (R): Light travels faster in an optically denser medium.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 2, explain: "n of water (1.33) is less than n of glass (1.52), so v = c/n is greater in water: A is true. R is false: light travels more slowly in a denser medium.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Light passes from air into a medium. The angle of incidence is 60° and the angle of refraction is 30°. The refractive index of the medium and the speed of light in it are nearly:", options: ["2.00; 1.5 × 10⁸ m/s", "1.73; 1.73 × 10⁸ m/s", "0.58; 5.2 × 10⁸ m/s", "1.73; 5.2 × 10⁸ m/s"], answer: 1, explain: "n = sin 60°/sin 30° = 0.866/0.5 = 1.73. v = c/n = (3 × 10⁸)/1.73 = 1.73 × 10⁸ m/s.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Define the principal focus of a concave mirror.", a: "It is the point on the principal axis at which rays of light parallel to the principal axis, after reflection from the mirror, actually meet (converge).", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Write the relation between the refractive index of a medium and the speed of light in it.", a: "Refractive index n = speed of light in vacuum (c) ÷ speed of light in the medium (v), i.e. n = c/v.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Find the power of a lens of focal length −50 cm. Is it convex or concave?", a: "f = −50 cm = −0.5 m, so P = 1/f = 1/(−0.5) = −2 D.\nNegative power means a concave lens.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "State two characteristics of the image formed by a plane mirror.", a: "The image is virtual and erect, of the same size as the object.\nIt is formed as far behind the mirror as the object is in front, and it is laterally inverted (any two).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "A concave mirror forms a real image three times the size of an object placed 12 cm from it. Find the image distance and the focal length of the mirror.", a: "u = −12 cm; real image means m = −3. m = −v/u gives −3 = −v/(−12), so v = −36 cm (36 cm in front of the mirror).\n1/f = 1/v + 1/u = −1/36 − 1/12 = −4/36, so f = −9 cm.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "The refractive index of diamond is 2.42. Calculate the speed of light in diamond. (c = 3 × 10⁸ m/s)", a: "v = c/n = (3 × 10⁸)/2.42\n= 1.24 × 10⁸ m/s.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "A convex lens has a focal length of 25 cm. An object is kept 40 cm from it. Using the lens formula with the New Cartesian sign convention, find the position and nature of the image and the magnification.", a: "Convention: distances are measured from the optical centre; the direction of incident light is positive. So u = −40 cm and f = +25 cm.\n1/v = 1/f + 1/u = 1/25 − 1/40 = (8 − 5)/200 = 3/200, so v = +66.7 cm (on the other side of the lens).\nm = v/u = 66.7/(−40) ≈ −1.67.\nThe image is real, inverted and enlarged, formed 66.7 cm from the lens.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A 6 cm tall object is placed 90 cm in front of a convex mirror of focal length 30 cm. Find the image distance, magnification and the size of the image. State the nature of the image.", a: "u = −90 cm, f = +30 cm (convex mirror).\n1/v = 1/f − 1/u = 1/30 + 1/90 = 4/90, so v = +22.5 cm (behind the mirror).\nm = −v/u = −22.5/(−90) = +0.25; h′ = m × h = 0.25 × 6 = +1.5 cm.\nThe image is virtual, erect and diminished (1.5 cm tall).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Describe in words the path of a ray of light through a rectangular glass slab. Why is the emergent ray parallel to the incident ray, and why is it laterally displaced?", a: "A ray entering the glass (rarer to denser) bends towards the normal; on leaving the slab (denser to rarer) it bends away from the normal.\nThe two refractions are equal and opposite because the two faces are parallel, so the emergent ray is parallel to the incident ray.\nThe ray travels sideways inside the slab, so the emergent ray is shifted sideways, called lateral displacement.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "State the position, nature and relative size of the image formed by a concave mirror when the object is placed (a) beyond C, (b) at C, (c) between C and F, (d) at F, (e) between P and F.", a: "(a) Beyond C: image between F and C; real, inverted, diminished.\n(b) At C: image at C; real, inverted, same size.\n(c) Between C and F: image beyond C; real, inverted, enlarged.\n(d) At F: image at infinity; real, inverted, highly enlarged.\n(e) Between P and F: image behind the mirror; virtual, erect, enlarged.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "A 4 cm tall object is placed 20 cm in front of a concave mirror of radius of curvature 30 cm. (a) Find the position, nature and size of the image. (b) At what distance from the mirror should the object be placed to get a virtual image twice its size? Use the New Cartesian sign convention.", a: "Convention: distances are measured from the pole, positive along the direction of incident light. Concave mirror: f = −R/2 = −15 cm; u = −20 cm.\n(a) 1/v = 1/f − 1/u = −1/15 + 1/20 = (−4 + 3)/60 = −1/60, so v = −60 cm (60 cm in front of the mirror).\nm = −v/u = −(−60)/(−20) = −3; h′ = −3 × 4 = −12 cm.\nThe image is real, inverted and enlarged, 12 cm tall.\n(b) Virtual image twice the size: m = +2, so −v/u = 2 and v = −2u.\n1/v + 1/u = 1/f gives −1/(2u) + 1/u = 1/(2u) = −1/15, so u = −7.5 cm.\nThe object should be 7.5 cm from the mirror (between P and F); v = +15 cm behind the mirror.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Case study: A student places a candle flame 2 cm tall at 20 cm from a convex lens of focal length 15 cm and moves a screen until a sharp image forms. She then moves the candle to 10 cm from the lens.\n(i) At what distance from the lens is the screen for the first position?\n(ii) What is the size and nature of the image in the first position?\n(iii) What is seen when the candle is at 10 cm? Can it be caught on the screen?", a: "(i) u = −20 cm, f = +15 cm: 1/v = 1/15 − 1/20 = 1/60, so v = +60 cm. The screen is 60 cm from the lens, on the other side.\n(ii) m = v/u = 60/(−20) = −3; h′ = −3 × 2 = −6 cm. The image is real, inverted and enlarged, 6 cm tall.\n(iii) u = −10 cm: 1/v = 1/15 − 1/10 = −1/30, so v = −30 cm and m = +3. The image is virtual, erect and enlarged, on the same side as the candle; it cannot be caught on a screen (the object is between F and O, like a magnifying glass).", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "human-eye-and-colourful-world": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The part of the eye that controls the size of the pupil is the:", options: ["cornea", "retina", "iris", "ciliary muscles"], answer: 2, explain: "The iris is a dark muscular diaphragm that widens or narrows the pupil to regulate the light entering the eye.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which defect of vision is corrected using a concave lens?", options: ["Myopia", "Hypermetropia", "Presbyopia", "Cataract"], answer: 0, explain: "In myopia the image forms in front of the retina; a concave lens diverges the rays so the image moves back onto the retina.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The image in the human eye is formed on the:", options: ["pupil", "cornea", "eye lens", "retina"], answer: 3, explain: "The retina is the light-sensitive screen that receives the real, inverted image.", level: 1 },
      { q: "When white light passes through a glass prism, the colour that bends the least is:", options: ["violet", "red", "green", "blue"], answer: 1, explain: "Red bends the least and violet the most.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The near point of a normal human eye is about:", options: ["25 cm", "25 m", "2.5 cm", "at infinity"], answer: 0, explain: "The least distance of distinct vision for a normal eye is about 25 cm.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The sky appears blue in daytime because:", options: ["it reflects the colour of the sea", "clouds disperse sunlight", "ozone absorbs red light", "air molecules scatter blue light more than red"], answer: 3, explain: "Fine air molecules scatter shorter wavelengths (blue) much more strongly than longer ones (red).", level: 1 },

      { q: "In a hypermetropic eye, the image of a nearby object is formed:", options: ["in front of the retina", "behind the retina", "exactly on the retina", "on the cornea"], answer: 1, explain: "The eye lens is too weak (or the eyeball too short), so the rays are not bent enough and meet behind the retina.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Planets do not twinkle because:", options: ["they are much farther away than stars", "they produce their own steady light", "they are nearby and act as extended sources, so variations average out", "the atmosphere does not refract their light"], answer: 2, explain: "A planet is a collection of point sources; the changes in refraction average out, so the light seems steady.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Arun cannot read the writing on the blackboard from the last bench but can read his book easily. Which defect does he have and which lens is needed?", options: ["Hypermetropia; convex lens", "Presbyopia; bifocal lens", "Cataract; surgery", "Myopia; concave lens"], answer: 3, explain: "Blurred distant vision with clear near vision is myopia, corrected by a concave lens.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The Sun appears reddish at sunrise and sunset because:", options: ["its light travels a longer path through the air, so most blue light is scattered away", "red light is scattered the most", "violet light is absorbed by the Sun", "the Sun is cooler in the morning and evening"], answer: 0, explain: "Near the horizon, sunlight passes through more air, so blue is scattered out and mainly red reaches the eye.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Danger signals are red in colour.\nReason (R): Red light has the longest wavelength among the visible colours and is scattered the least by fog or smoke.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Because red is scattered least, it can be seen from the farthest in fog or smoke.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Neha, a young girl, sees distant objects clearly but has to hold her book far away to read. The doctor says her near point is beyond 25 cm. Her defect and the lens prescribed are:", options: ["myopia; concave lens", "presbyopia; concave lens", "hypermetropia; convex lens", "cataract; convex lens"], answer: 2, explain: "A near point farther than 25 cm is hypermetropia, corrected with a convex lens.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A rainbow is formed by:", options: ["dispersion only, without refraction", "dispersion, refraction and internal reflection of sunlight in water droplets", "scattering and absorption in clouds", "reflection of sunlight from the sea"], answer: 1, explain: "Tiny droplets act like small prisms: light is refracted and dispersed, internally reflected, and refracted again as it leaves.", level: 2 },

      { q: "A person can see clearly only up to 50 cm. The power of the lens needed to see distant objects clearly is:", options: ["−0.5 D", "+2 D", "−2 D", "+0.5 D"], answer: 2, explain: "The lens must form an image of a distant object at 50 cm: f = −50 cm = −0.5 m, so P = 1/f = −2 D.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A rainbow is seen in the morning. The observer must be facing:", options: ["east", "west", "north", "south"], answer: 1, explain: "A rainbow is seen opposite to the Sun, and in the morning the Sun is in the east, so the rainbow is in the west.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Stars twinkle because they emit light in pulses.\nReason (R): The light of a star is continuously refracted by the changing layers of the atmosphere.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "Stars do not emit pulses of light. Twinkling is due to atmospheric refraction, which keeps changing the apparent position and brightness of the star.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Because of atmospheric refraction, the Sun is visible about 2 minutes before actual sunrise and 2 minutes after actual sunset. The day is therefore lengthened by about:", options: ["4 minutes", "2 minutes", "8 minutes", "1 minute"], answer: 0, explain: "2 minutes gained at sunrise + 2 minutes at sunset = about 4 minutes.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A hypermetropic person has a near point at 40 cm. The power of the lens needed to read comfortably at 25 cm is:", options: ["+1.5 D", "+2.5 D", "−1.5 D", "+0.4 D"], answer: 0, explain: "The lens forms a virtual image of an object at u = −25 cm at v = −40 cm: 1/f = 1/v − 1/u = −1/40 + 1/25 = 3/200, so f = 66.7 cm and P = +1.5 D.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "What is the function of the ciliary muscles in the human eye?", a: "They change the curvature (thickness) of the eye lens, and therefore its focal length, so that the eye can focus on near and distant objects.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What is the Tyndall effect?", a: "The scattering of light by colloidal particles (such as dust, smoke or water droplets) in air, which makes the path of a beam of light visible.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Name the type of lens used to correct hypermetropia.", a: "A convex lens of suitable power.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Why do stars twinkle but planets do not?", a: "Starlight passes through layers of the atmosphere whose refractive index keeps changing, so the apparent position and brightness of the star fluctuate; a star is a point source, so it twinkles.\nA planet is much nearer and acts as an extended source (a collection of point sources), so the fluctuations average out and it does not twinkle.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "What are bifocal lenses? Why are they used by some elderly people?", a: "A bifocal lens has a concave upper part for distant vision and a convex lower part for near vision.\nOlder people may suffer from both myopia and hypermetropia (presbyopia), so one pair of spectacles corrects both.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Explain why the Sun is seen about 2 minutes before actual sunrise and for about 2 minutes after actual sunset.", a: "The refractive index of air increases towards the Earth, so sunlight bends towards the normal as it passes through the atmosphere.\nThis makes the Sun appear above the horizon even when it is just below it, giving advance sunrise and delayed sunset.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "What is myopia? State two causes, name the lens used to correct it and explain in words how the lens helps.", a: "Myopia (near-sightedness) is the defect in which a person sees nearby objects clearly but distant objects look blurred, because the image forms in front of the retina.\nCauses: excessive curvature of the eye lens, or elongation of the eyeball.\nA concave lens of suitable power diverges the rays slightly before they enter the eye, so the image shifts back and forms on the retina.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "The far point of a myopic person is 1.5 m. Find the nature and power of the lens needed to see distant objects clearly.", a: "The lens must form a virtual image of a very distant object (u = ∞) at the far point, so v = −1.5 m.\n1/f = 1/v − 1/u = −1/1.5 − 0, so f = −1.5 m.\nP = 1/f = −0.67 D (about −2/3 D): a concave lens is needed.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Describe how Newton showed that a prism does not add colours to sunlight but only splits it. What does the experiment prove?", a: "He passed a beam of sunlight through a glass prism and obtained a spectrum of seven colours (VIBGYOR).\nHe then placed a second identical prism in an inverted position next to the first; the spectrum recombined and white light emerged.\nThis proves that white light is made of seven colours and the prism only disperses them (red bends least, violet most).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A hypermetropic person has a near point at 75 cm. (a) What is hypermetropia and what are its causes? (b) Calculate the power of the lens needed to read a book kept at 25 cm. (c) Name the type of lens.", a: "(a) In hypermetropia a person sees distant objects clearly but nearby objects are blurred, because the near point is farther than 25 cm and the image forms behind the retina. Causes: the focal length of the eye lens is too long, or the eyeball is too small.\n(b) The lens must form a virtual image of the book at the near point: u = −25 cm, v = −75 cm.\n1/f = 1/v − 1/u = −1/75 + 1/25 = (−1 + 3)/75 = 2/75, so f = 37.5 cm = 0.375 m.\nP = 1/f = +2.67 D.\n(c) A convex lens (positive power).", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Explain the formation of a rainbow. Why is it seen only when the Sun is behind the observer?", a: "A rainbow is formed in the sky after rain, when tiny water droplets remain in the air.\nThe droplets act like small prisms. Sunlight is refracted and dispersed as it enters a droplet.\nThe dispersed light is internally reflected inside the droplet and refracted again as it leaves, so the colours separate further.\nDifferent colours reach the eye from different droplets, giving a band of VIBGYOR with red on the outer side.\nThe light is sent back towards the side from which it came, so the rainbow is seen in the direction opposite to the Sun, with the observer's back to the Sun.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Case study: Rohan, 13, finds that he cannot read the board from the last bench, though he can read his book easily. An eye test shows that the farthest point he can see clearly is 40 cm from his eye.\n(i) Name the defect he has and state its two possible causes.\n(ii) Which type of lens will correct it?\n(iii) Calculate the power of the lens he needs.", a: "(i) Myopia (near-sightedness). Causes: excessive curvature of the eye lens, or an elongated eyeball.\n(ii) A concave lens.\n(iii) The lens must form an image of a distant object at the far point: v = −40 cm, u = ∞, so f = −40 cm = −0.4 m.\nP = 1/f = 1/(−0.4) = −2.5 D.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "electricity": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The SI unit of electrical resistance is the:", options: ["volt", "ampere", "ohm", "watt"], answer: 2, explain: "Resistance R = V/I is measured in ohms (Ω), where 1 Ω = 1 V/A.", level: 1 },
      { q: "An ammeter is connected in a circuit:", options: ["in parallel with the component", "in series with the component", "either in series or in parallel", "only across the battery"], answer: 1, explain: "An ammeter has very low resistance and measures the current through the circuit, so it is joined in series.", level: 1 },
      { q: "One kilowatt hour (1 kW h) is equal to:", options: ["3.6 × 10⁶ J", "3.6 × 10³ J", "1000 J", "6.3 × 10⁶ J"], answer: 0, explain: "1 kW h = 1000 W × 3600 s = 3.6 × 10⁶ J.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The SI unit of electric charge is the:", options: ["ampere", "volt", "joule", "coulomb"], answer: 3, explain: "Charge is measured in coulombs (C); 1 A = 1 C/s.", level: 1 },
      { q: "When resistors are joined in series, the total resistance is:", options: ["less than the smallest resistance", "equal to the largest resistance", "greater than the largest resistance", "the average of all the resistances"], answer: 2, explain: "In series Rₛ = R₁ + R₂ + R₃ + …, which is greater than any single resistance.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Household electrical appliances are connected in:", options: ["parallel", "series", "series–parallel only", "a star arrangement"], answer: 0, explain: "In parallel, each appliance gets the full 220 V and can be switched on or off independently.", level: 1 },

      { q: "A wire of resistance R is cut into two equal halves, and the two halves are joined in parallel. The resultant resistance is:", options: ["R/2", "R/4", "2R", "4R"], answer: 1, explain: "Each half has R/2. Two equal resistances R/2 in parallel give (R/2)/2 = R/4.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Two bulbs rated 100 W and 60 W are designed for the same 220 V supply. Which statement is correct?", options: ["Both have equal resistance", "The 100 W bulb has the greater resistance", "Their resistances cannot be compared", "The 60 W bulb has the greater resistance"], answer: 3, explain: "R = V²/P, so for the same V the bulb with the smaller power has the larger resistance.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Rakesh plugged a 2 kW heater into a socket protected by a 5 A fuse on a 220 V supply, and the fuse blew. The reason is that:", options: ["the heater has a very high resistance", "the supply voltage dropped suddenly", "the heater draws about 9 A, more than the 5 A rating of the fuse", "the fuse was connected in parallel with the heater"], answer: 2, explain: "I = P/V = 2000/220 ≈ 9.1 A, which is greater than 5 A, so the fuse melted.", level: 2 },
      { q: "In a graph of potential difference V (on the vertical axis) against current I (on the horizontal axis) for a resistor, the slope of the straight line gives its:", options: ["power", "resistance", "charge", "resistivity"], answer: 1, explain: "By Ohm's law V = IR, so V/I = R is the slope.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Nichrome is used as the heating element in electric heaters.\nReason (R): Nichrome has a high resistivity and does not oxidise (burn) easily when red hot.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "A high resistivity gives more heat, and resistance to oxidation lets the element last long at high temperature.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "An electric kettle of rating 1500 W is used for 20 minutes every day. At ₹6 per unit, its cost for 30 days is:", options: ["₹60", "₹270", "₹45", "₹90"], answer: 3, explain: "Energy per day = 1.5 kW × (1/3) h = 0.5 kW h; for 30 days = 15 units. Cost = 15 × 6 = ₹90.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Two resistors of 4 Ω and 12 Ω are connected in parallel. The equivalent resistance is:", options: ["16 Ω", "8 Ω", "3 Ω", "0.33 Ω"], answer: 2, explain: "1/Rₚ = 1/4 + 1/12 = 4/12, so Rₚ = 3 Ω.", level: 2 },

      { q: "Resistors of 6 Ω and 3 Ω are connected in parallel, and this combination is joined in series with a 4 Ω resistor and a 12 V battery. The current drawn from the battery is:", options: ["1 A", "3 A", "2 A", "4 A"], answer: 2, explain: "The parallel part is (6 × 3)/(6 + 3) = 2 Ω. Total resistance = 2 + 4 = 6 Ω, so I = 12/6 = 2 A.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A heating element carries a current that is halved while its resistance is doubled, for the same time. The heat produced becomes:", options: ["the same", "one-fourth", "double", "half"], answer: 3, explain: "H = I²Rt. New H = (I/2)² × 2R × t = (1/4) × 2 × I²Rt = half.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A wire of resistance R is replaced by a wire of the same material with double the length and double the radius. The new resistance is:", options: ["R/2", "R", "2R", "4R"], answer: 0, explain: "R = ρl/A. Length ×2 doubles R, but area ×4 divides it by 4, so the new resistance is 2/4 R = R/2.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): A fuse wire is connected in series with an electrical appliance.\nReason (R): A fuse wire is made of a material with a very high melting point.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 2, explain: "A fuse is in series so that the whole current flows through it, but it has a low melting point so that it melts when the current becomes too large.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Resistors of 10 Ω and 20 Ω are connected in series for the same time. The ratio of heat produced in the 10 Ω resistor to that in the 20 Ω resistor is:", options: ["2 : 1", "1 : 2", "1 : 1", "4 : 1"], answer: 1, explain: "The same current flows in series, so H = I²Rt is proportional to R: 10 : 20 = 1 : 2.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Write the SI unit of resistivity.", a: "Ohm metre (Ω m).", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Why is tungsten used for the filament of an electric bulb?", a: "Tungsten has a very high melting point (3380 °C), so it can glow white hot without melting, and it has high resistivity.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "How is a voltmeter connected in a circuit? Give a reason.", a: "In parallel across the component, because it measures the potential difference between two points and has a very high resistance, so it draws almost no current.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Why are electrical appliances in a house not connected in series? Give two reasons.", a: "In series, if one appliance fails or is switched off, the circuit breaks and all the other appliances stop working.\nThe voltage is shared among the appliances, so each gets less than 220 V and cannot work properly (the current is the same in all).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "A piece of wire of resistance 12 Ω is cut into three equal parts, and the three parts are connected in parallel. Find the resultant resistance.", a: "Each part has resistance 12/3 = 4 Ω.\n1/Rₚ = 1/4 + 1/4 + 1/4 = 3/4, so Rₚ = 4/3 Ω ≈ 1.33 Ω.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "An electric heater of resistance 50 Ω carries a current of 4 A for 5 minutes. Calculate the heat produced.", a: "H = I²Rt, with t = 5 × 60 = 300 s.\nH = (4)² × 50 × 300 = 2.4 × 10⁵ J.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "A 4 Ω and a 12 Ω resistor are connected in parallel, and this combination is joined in series with a 5 Ω resistor across a 12 V battery. Find (a) the total resistance, (b) the current from the battery, (c) the current through the 12 Ω resistor.", a: "(a) Parallel part: 1/R = 1/4 + 1/12 = 4/12, so R = 3 Ω. Total R = 3 + 5 = 8 Ω.\n(b) I = V/R = 12/8 = 1.5 A.\n(c) Potential difference across the parallel part = 1.5 × 3 = 4.5 V. Current in the 12 Ω resistor = 4.5/12 = 0.375 A (the 4 Ω carries 1.125 A; 0.375 + 1.125 = 1.5 A).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Why is a 5 A fuse chosen for a 1 kW electric iron working on a 220 V supply? Explain how a fuse protects the iron.", a: "Current drawn by the iron: I = P/V = 1000/220 ≈ 4.5 A.\nFuse ratings available are 1, 2, 3, 5, 10 A; the smallest rating above 4.5 A is 5 A, so a 5 A fuse allows normal working but melts if the current rises above it.\nThe fuse is in series with the iron; when excess current flows, the heat melts the fuse wire, which breaks the circuit and protects the iron.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Wire A has length l and radius r. Wire B is of the same material with length 2l and radius 2r. Find the ratio of the resistance of A to that of B.", a: "R = ρl/A, A = πr².\nR_A = ρl/(πr²).\nR_B = ρ(2l)/(π(2r)²) = ρ(2l)/(4πr²) = ρl/(2πr²).\nR_A : R_B = [1/(πr²)] : [1/(2πr²)] = 2 : 1.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Derive the formula for the equivalent resistance of three resistors R₁, R₂ and R₃ connected in parallel.", a: "Let the resistors be joined between two points with a potential difference V; V is the same across each.\nCurrents: I₁ = V/R₁, I₂ = V/R₂, I₃ = V/R₃.\nThe total current I splits at the junction, so I = I₁ + I₂ + I₃ = V(1/R₁ + 1/R₂ + 1/R₃).\nIf Rₚ is the single equivalent resistance, I = V/Rₚ.\nComparing, 1/Rₚ = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂ + 1/R₃.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Resistors of 6 Ω and 3 Ω are connected in parallel; this combination is in series with a 5 Ω resistor on one side and a 3 Ω resistor on the other, and the whole circuit is connected to a 20 V battery. Find (a) the total resistance, (b) the current from the battery, (c) the potential difference across the parallel combination, (d) the current in each of the 6 Ω and 3 Ω resistors, (e) the power dissipated in the 3 Ω series resistor.", a: "(a) Parallel part: 1/R = 1/6 + 1/3 = 3/6, so R = 2 Ω. Total R = 5 + 2 + 3 = 10 Ω.\n(b) I = V/R = 20/10 = 2 A.\n(c) V across the parallel part = I × 2 Ω = 4 V.\n(d) Current in 6 Ω = 4/6 = 0.67 A; current in 3 Ω = 4/3 = 1.33 A (sum = 2 A).\n(e) P = I²R = 2² × 3 = 12 W.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Case study: A room has a bulb of 100 W, a fan of 60 W and a heater of 1.5 kW, all rated for 220 V and connected to the mains. The electrician has a choice of 5 A and 10 A fuses.\n(i) How are the appliances connected, and why?\n(ii) What is the total current drawn when all are on?\n(iii) Which fuse should be used? Why?", a: "(i) In parallel, so that each gets the full 220 V and can be switched on or off independently.\n(ii) Total power = 100 + 60 + 1500 = 1660 W. I = P/V = 1660/220 ≈ 7.5 A.\n(iii) A 10 A fuse. A 5 A fuse would melt at 7.5 A, while a 10 A fuse is the smallest rating above the normal current and still protects the circuit from overloading.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "magnetic-effects-of-electric-current": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Who first showed that a current-carrying wire deflects a nearby compass needle?", options: ["Ampère", "Faraday", "Oersted", "Fleming"], answer: 2, explain: "Hans Christian Oersted's observation linked electricity and magnetism.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Outside a bar magnet, the magnetic field lines go from:", options: ["south pole to north pole", "north pole to south pole", "east to west", "the middle of the magnet to its ends"], answer: 1, explain: "Field lines emerge from the north pole and enter the south pole outside the magnet, and continue from south to north inside.", level: 1 },
      { q: "In household wiring, the insulation of the live wire is usually:", options: ["green", "black", "blue", "red"], answer: 3, explain: "Live wire: red; neutral: black; earth: green.", level: 1 },
      { q: "In Fleming's left-hand rule, the thumb points in the direction of:", options: ["force (motion) on the conductor", "current", "magnetic field", "potential difference"], answer: 0, explain: "Forefinger = field, middle finger = current, thumb = force.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The insulation colour of the earth wire is:", options: ["red", "black", "green", "yellow"], answer: 2, explain: "The earth wire has green insulation.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The potential difference between the live and neutral wires of the domestic supply in India is:", options: ["110 V", "220 V", "440 V", "11 V"], answer: 1, explain: "The household supply is 220 V.", level: 1 },

      { q: "The magnetic field inside a long current-carrying solenoid is represented by:", options: ["radial lines", "concentric circles", "no lines (the field is zero)", "parallel straight lines"], answer: 3, explain: "Inside a solenoid the field is uniform, shown by parallel straight lines.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A current-carrying conductor placed in a magnetic field experiences no force when the current is:", options: ["perpendicular to the field", "parallel to the field", "at 45° to the field", "very large"], answer: 1, explain: "The force is maximum when current and field are perpendicular and zero when they are parallel.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A compass needle placed under a straight wire deflects when current flows. If the direction of current is reversed, the needle will:", options: ["deflect in the opposite direction", "show no change in deflection", "stop moving altogether", "deflect twice as much in the same direction"], answer: 0, explain: "Reversing the current reverses the direction of its magnetic field, so the needle deflects the other way.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The metal body of an electric iron accidentally touches the live wire. A person touching the iron does not get a severe shock because:", options: ["the rubber insulation of the handle protects him", "the neutral wire carries the current away", "the earth wire gives a low-resistance path so the leakage current goes to the ground", "the fuse in the neutral wire melts"], answer: 2, explain: "The earthed body stays at earth potential, and the large current to earth also blows the fuse.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Two magnetic field lines never cross each other.\nReason (R): At a point of intersection, a compass needle would have to point in two directions at the same time.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "The field at a point has one direction only, so lines cannot intersect.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "A straight vertical wire carries current upward. Looking from above, the magnetic field lines around it are:", options: ["clockwise circles", "anticlockwise circles", "radially outward lines", "vertical straight lines"], answer: 1, explain: "By the right-hand thumb rule, with the thumb pointing up the fingers curl anticlockwise as seen from above.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The strength of the magnetic field at the centre of a circular coil can be increased by:", options: ["decreasing the number of turns", "reducing the current", "increasing the number of turns", "using a longer wire for connection"], answer: 2, explain: "The field at the centre grows with the current and with the number of turns of the coil.", level: 2 },

      { q: "A uniform magnetic field points towards the north. A straight wire carries current towards the east. The force on the wire is directed:", options: ["towards the south", "downward", "upward", "towards the west"], answer: 2, explain: "Fleming's left-hand rule: forefinger points north (field), middle finger east (current); the thumb then points upward (force).", level: 3 },
      { q: "A 15 A domestic circuit on a 220 V supply already has a 2 kW geyser and a 1 kW iron working. The largest additional appliance that can be switched on without exceeding the circuit rating is about:", options: ["3300 W", "2200 W", "300 W", "500 W"], answer: 2, explain: "Maximum power = 15 × 220 = 3300 W. Already used = 3000 W. Remaining = 300 W.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): A current-carrying conductor placed parallel to a magnetic field experiences the maximum force.\nReason (R): The force on a current-carrying conductor is maximum when the current is perpendicular to the field.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "The force is zero when the conductor is parallel to the field, so A is false; R is true.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "A rod carrying current in a magnetic field experiences a force towards the left. If the directions of both the current and the magnetic field are reversed, the force will be:", options: ["towards the right", "unchanged, towards the left", "zero", "doubled"], answer: 1, explain: "The force direction depends on the product of the two directions. Reversing both leaves the force unchanged.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A 1.5 kW heater works on 220 V mains. Which fuse is most suitable from 1 A, 5 A, 15 A and 10 A?", options: ["1 A", "5 A", "15 A", "10 A"], answer: 3, explain: "I = 1500/220 ≈ 6.8 A. The 1 A and 5 A fuses would melt; 15 A is much higher than needed and gives poor protection. 10 A is suitable.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Name the device made by placing a soft iron core inside a solenoid.", a: "An electromagnet.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Why can two magnetic field lines never intersect?", a: "If they did, a compass needle at the point of intersection would have to point in two directions at once, which is not possible, since the field at a point has one direction.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "State the direction of magnetic field lines inside a bar magnet.", a: "From the south pole to the north pole (they form closed loops).", marks: 1 },
      { q: "State Fleming's left-hand rule.", a: "Stretch the thumb, forefinger and middle finger of the left hand at right angles to one another.\nIf the forefinger points in the direction of the magnetic field and the middle finger in the direction of the current, the thumb gives the direction of the force (motion) on the conductor.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Distinguish between short-circuiting and overloading of an electric circuit.", a: "Short-circuit: the live and neutral wires touch directly (e.g. due to damaged insulation), so the resistance is very small and the current becomes very large.\nOverloading: too many high-power appliances are connected to a single socket, or the supply voltage rises accidentally, so the current exceeds the safe limit.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Describe the magnetic field lines around a straight current-carrying wire. How does the field change with current and with distance from the wire?", a: "The field lines are concentric circles around the wire in a plane perpendicular to it; their direction is given by the right-hand thumb rule.\nThe field becomes stronger as the current increases and weaker as the distance from the wire increases.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Describe Oersted's experiment. What did it prove?", a: "A straight wire is held over a compass needle (parallel to it) and connected to a battery through a switch.\nWhen current flows, the needle is deflected; on reversing the current the deflection is reversed, and when the current stops the needle returns to its original position.\nThis proves that a current-carrying conductor produces a magnetic field around it, linking electricity and magnetism.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "What is a solenoid? Draw in words its magnetic field pattern and state two ways of increasing the field strength.", a: "A solenoid is a coil of many circular turns of insulated copper wire wound closely in the shape of a cylinder.\nThe field outside resembles that of a bar magnet (one end acts as north, the other as south); inside, the field lines are parallel straight lines, so the field is uniform.\nThe field can be increased by increasing the current and by increasing the number of turns (or by inserting a soft iron core).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Name the three wires of a domestic electric circuit with the colours of their insulation. State the function of the earth wire.", a: "Live wire: red insulation. Neutral wire: black insulation. Earth wire: green insulation.\nThe earth wire is connected to the metal body of an appliance and to a metal plate deep in the earth.\nIt gives a low-resistance path for any leakage current, so the user does not get an electric shock.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Describe an activity to show that a magnetic field exerts a force on a current-carrying conductor. State how the force changes when the current or field is reversed, and give two devices that use this effect.", a: "Set-up: hang a small aluminium rod horizontally from two conducting wires between the poles of a horseshoe magnet, so the field is vertical. Connect the rod to a battery through a switch and rheostat.\nObservation: on passing current the rod is displaced (moves sideways); this shows that a force acts on it.\nReversing the direction of the current moves the rod in the opposite direction; reversing the magnet's poles also reverses the displacement.\nThe force is greatest when the rod is perpendicular to the field; its direction is given by Fleming's left-hand rule.\nDevices: electric motor, loudspeaker (also galvanometer, microphone).", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Describe the domestic electric circuit. Include the wires from the mains, the two separate circuits, how appliances are connected, the safety devices and their purposes.", a: "Mains supply comes through a live wire (red) and a neutral wire (black); the potential difference between them is 220 V. An earth wire (green) is also provided.\nTwo separate circuits are used: a 15 A circuit for high-power appliances (geyser, air cooler) and a 5 A circuit for bulbs and fans.\nAppliances are connected in parallel across the live and neutral wires, each with its own switch, so each gets 220 V and works independently.\nThe earth wire is joined to the metal body of appliances, so any leakage current passes to the ground and prevents shocks.\nAn electric fuse in series with the live wire melts and breaks the circuit when excess current flows (short-circuit or overloading), protecting the wiring and appliances.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Case study: Rahul winds a long insulated copper wire around an iron nail and connects the ends to a battery through a switch. When the switch is closed, the nail picks up some iron pins. When the switch is opened, the pins fall off.\n(i) Name the device Rahul has made.\n(ii) Why do the pins fall when the switch is opened?\n(iii) Suggest two ways to make it pick up more pins.", a: "(i) An electromagnet (a solenoid with a soft iron core).\n(ii) The magnetic field exists only while current flows. When the current stops, the soft iron core loses its magnetism, so the pins fall.\n(iii) Increase the number of turns of the coil, and increase the current by adding more cells (any two).", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "our-environment": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Which of the following is a biotic component of an ecosystem?", options: ["Soil", "Temperature", "Fungi", "Rainfall"], answer: 2, explain: "Living things such as fungi are biotic; soil, temperature and rainfall are abiotic.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of these organisms is a producer?", options: ["Grasshopper", "Green plant", "Frog", "Fungus"], answer: 1, explain: "Green plants make food by photosynthesis, so they are producers.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A molecule of ozone is made up of how many oxygen atoms?", options: ["1", "2", "4", "3"], answer: 3, explain: "Ozone is O₃; ordinary oxygen is O₂.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of these is non-biodegradable?", options: ["Plastic", "Paper", "Wood", "Cotton"], answer: 0, explain: "Microorganisms cannot break down plastics; paper, wood and cotton are biodegradable.", level: 1 },
      { q: "According to the 10 per cent law, the energy passed on from one trophic level to the next is about:", options: ["1%", "10%", "50%", "90%"], answer: 1, explain: "Only about 10% of the energy at a level is available to the next level.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The ozone layer is mainly damaged by:", options: ["carbon dioxide", "sulphur dioxide", "chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs)", "methane"], answer: 2, explain: "CFCs from refrigerants and aerosols release chlorine that breaks down ozone.", level: 1 },

      { q: "In the food chain grass → insect → frog → snake → hawk, the maximum concentration of a non-biodegradable pesticide will be found in:", options: ["grass", "insect", "snake", "hawk"], answer: 3, explain: "Biological magnification increases the concentration at each higher trophic level, so it is greatest in the hawk.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The ozone layer in the upper atmosphere is useful because it:", options: ["absorbs the harmful ultraviolet radiation of the Sun", "reflects infrared radiation", "releases oxygen for breathing", "absorbs carbon dioxide"], answer: 0, explain: "Ozone shields living things from UV radiation, which causes skin cancer and other damage.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A farmer sprays a lot of pesticide on his crops. Years later, fish-eating birds in a nearby lake are found to have the highest level of the chemical in their bodies. This phenomenon is called:", options: ["decomposition", "biological magnification", "ozone depletion", "photosynthesis"], answer: 1, explain: "Non-biodegradable chemicals accumulate progressively along the food chain.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which of the following is a human-made (artificial) ecosystem?", options: ["Forest", "Pond", "Aquarium", "Lake"], answer: 2, explain: "Gardens, crop fields and aquariums are artificial; forests, ponds and lakes are natural.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Food chains rarely have more than four or five trophic levels.\nReason (R): Only about 10% of the energy of one trophic level is passed to the next level.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Energy falls to a tenth at each step, so very little is left to support organisms at higher levels.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "A school keeps two bins for waste. Which set of items belongs entirely in the non-biodegradable bin?", options: ["Banana peel and tea leaves", "Plastic wrapper and glass bottle", "Cotton cloth and polythene bag", "Newspaper and DDT"], answer: 1, explain: "Plastic and glass are not broken down by microorganisms. The other sets each contain at least one biodegradable item.", level: 2 },
      { q: "In an ecosystem, the number of herbivores is usually less than the number of producers because:", options: ["herbivores are larger", "herbivores eat only a few plants", "energy decreases at each higher level, so fewer organisms can be supported", "producers are eaten by decomposers"], answer: 2, explain: "Since energy falls by about 90% at each step, each level supports fewer organisms than the one below.", level: 2 },

      { q: "A food chain has four trophic levels. If the producers receive 20,000 J of energy, the energy available at the fourth trophic level is nearly:", options: ["2000 J", "20 J", "200 J", "2 J"], answer: 1, explain: "Level 2 = 2000 J, level 3 = 200 J, level 4 = 20 J (10% at each step).", level: 3 },
      { q: "Compared with eating grain directly, a person who eats a chicken that was fed on that grain gets nearly how much of the grain's energy?", options: ["half", "ten times as much", "one-tenth", "the same"], answer: 2, explain: "An extra trophic level is added, and only about 10% of energy passes on, so the person gets about one-tenth of what grain would give.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Biological magnification is highest in top carnivores.\nReason (R): The pesticides that enter food chains are biodegradable and are excreted quickly.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 2, explain: "A is true. R is false: these chemicals are non-biodegradable, so they accumulate instead of being removed.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "What would happen if all the decomposers in an ecosystem were removed?", options: ["Dead remains would pile up and nutrients would not return to the soil", "Energy would flow faster through the food chain", "The number of producers would increase greatly", "The ozone layer would be repaired"], answer: 0, explain: "Decomposers break dead matter into simple substances and return nutrients to the soil; without them the cycle stops.", level: 3 },
      { q: "In the food chain grass → grasshopper → frog → snake → eagle, all frogs are suddenly killed by a disease. The most likely effect is that:", options: ["both grasshoppers and snakes increase", "grasshoppers decrease and snakes increase", "there is no change at all", "grasshoppers increase and snakes decrease"], answer: 3, explain: "Fewer frogs means fewer grasshoppers are eaten (they increase), and snakes lose their food (they decrease).", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "What is a trophic level?", a: "Each step in a food chain at which food (energy) is transferred, e.g. producers, herbivores, carnivores.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Name the chemicals mainly responsible for the depletion of the ozone layer.", a: "Chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs).", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What is the role of decomposers in an ecosystem?", a: "Decomposers (bacteria and fungi) break down dead plants, animals and wastes into simple substances, returning nutrients to the soil.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Distinguish between biodegradable and non-biodegradable substances. Give one example of each.", a: "Biodegradable substances are broken down by microorganisms (e.g. vegetable peels, paper).\nNon-biodegradable substances are not broken down and persist in the environment (e.g. plastic, DDT, glass).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Why is the flow of energy in an ecosystem unidirectional?", a: "The energy captured by producers from sunlight passes to herbivores and then to carnivores, and does not return to the Sun or to the previous level.\nAt each step a large part is lost as heat in life processes, so it cannot be reused by the earlier level.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "How is ozone formed in the upper atmosphere? Write the reactions.", a: "Ultraviolet radiation splits oxygen molecules into free oxygen atoms: O₂ → O + O.\nThese atoms combine with oxygen molecules to form ozone: O + O₂ → O₃.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "In the food chain grass → grasshopper → frog → snake, the grass has 20,000 J of energy available. Find the energy available to each of the other levels.", a: "By the 10% law, each level gets 10% of the previous one.\nGrasshopper: 10% of 20,000 = 2000 J.\nFrog: 10% of 2000 = 200 J.\nSnake: 10% of 200 = 20 J.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "What is biological magnification? Explain how it occurs and why humans are most affected.", a: "Biological magnification is the progressive increase in the concentration of harmful non-biodegradable chemicals at each higher trophic level of a food chain.\nPesticides sprayed on crops enter the soil and water, are absorbed by plants, and are not broken down or excreted, so they build up as they pass from one organism to the next.\nHumans are at the top of many food chains, so the maximum concentration collects in our bodies, e.g. through pesticide residues in food grains, vegetables and milk.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Why is it important to segregate our waste into biodegradable and non-biodegradable? Suggest two ways of reducing plastic waste.", a: "Biodegradable waste can be broken down by microorganisms (e.g. composted), while non-biodegradable waste needs separate recycling or safe disposal, so segregation makes waste management easier and prevents pollution.\nUsing cloth or jute bags instead of plastic bags, and avoiding single-use plastics (reuse and recycle containers), reduce plastic waste.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Describe the components of an ecosystem. Explain the role of producers, consumers and decomposers and differentiate between natural and artificial ecosystems.", a: "An ecosystem has biotic components (plants, animals, microorganisms) and abiotic components (temperature, rainfall, wind, soil, minerals).\nProducers (green plants, blue-green algae) make food by photosynthesis.\nConsumers (herbivores, carnivores, omnivores, parasites) depend directly or indirectly on producers for food.\nDecomposers (bacteria and fungi) break down dead matter and return nutrients to the soil.\nNatural ecosystems are forests, ponds and lakes; artificial (human-made) ecosystems are gardens, crop fields and aquariums.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Explain the importance of the ozone layer, how it is formed and how it is being damaged. What action was taken to protect it?", a: "Importance: ozone in the upper atmosphere absorbs harmful ultraviolet (UV) radiation from the Sun, which can cause skin cancer and damage living organisms.\nFormation: UV splits O₂ into atoms, O₂ → O + O, and O + O₂ → O₃.\nDamage: chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs), used in refrigerators, air conditioners and fire extinguishers, release chlorine that destroys ozone and thins the layer.\nAction: in 1987 the United Nations Environment Programme (UNEP) got an agreement to freeze the production of CFCs at 1986 levels, and it is now mandatory for manufacturers to make CFC-free refrigerators.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Case study: In a study of a lake, a pesticide was measured (in arbitrary units) in water: 0.001, algae: 0.02, small fish: 0.5, large fish: 2 and fish-eating birds: 25.\n(i) In which organism is the concentration highest, and why?\n(ii) Name the phenomenon shown by the data.\n(iii) Why are humans at risk from such pesticides, and name one step to reduce the problem?", a: "(i) Fish-eating birds, since they are at the top trophic level and the chemical accumulates at each step.\n(ii) Biological magnification.\n(iii) Humans are also at the top of food chains, so they get a high concentration through food. Using less pesticide (organic farming, biological pest control) or washing and choosing safe produce reduces the problem.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  }
});
